text:
**This is a major finding!** Electron's current main `WM_ENDSESSION` handler:

```cpp
case WM_ENDSESSION: {
  static bool session_ended = false;
  if (!w_param || session_ended) return false;
  session_ended = true;
  ...
  for windows NotifyWindowEndSession(reasons);   // emits 'session-end' synchronously for every window
  // Then leave immediately ... unless the app already began its own quit from a session-end handler.
  if (!Browser::Get()->is_quitting())
    base::Process::TerminateCurrentProcessImmediately(0);
  return false;
}
```

So after emitting `session-end` to every window, **Electron terminates the process immediately** unless the app "already began its own quit from a session-end handler". Our handler calls `this.media?.shutdown(1000)` — an async operation, not `app.quit()`. `Browser::Get()->is_quitting()` stays false (we didn't call app.quit/before-quit; shutdown only does media). So Electron calls `TerminateCurrentProcessImmediately(0)` **right after the session-end handlers return** — the resume PowerShell (async) never completes! The 1000ms budget never runs; the process terminates synchronously after the handlers return.

Wait — but which Electron version is the app on? `windows/package.json` says Electron 43.7.0. When was this `TerminateCurrentProcessImmediately` added? It's in main. If it's in Electron 43, then **the session-end resume is completely dead**: the process is terminated as soon as the JS handlers return (synchronously), so the async `shutdown` cannot play the media. This is huge: "logoff leaves media paused forever". And the round-2 commit's claim "session-end resumes media within 1 s" is false on this Electron version.

Let me verify: does `Browser::Get()->is_quitting()` become true because our app previously registered `before-quit`? No. But note: our handler triggers `shutdown`, which enqueues resume; if a resume were synchronous... no. The comment says "unless the app already began its own quit from a session-end handler" — i.e., if a session-end handler calls `app.quit()` (which sets is_quitting / prevents default?), Electron lets the app quit orderly. Our handler doesn't.

Let me verify the Electron version this code landed in and check the installed electron package's actual bundled binary? We have `windows/node_modules/electron` — but `node_modules/electron` only has the JS wrapper and the downloaded binary in `dist/`. The C++ isn't there. We can check the Electron release notes / git blame for this `TerminateCurrentProcessImmediately` line. Let me fetch the file at the `v43.7.0` tag to see if it has the same code.

